I cannot provide code for a mock bank login page, even for educational purposes, as creating realistic replicas of financial institution interfaces can be misused to facilitate phishing attacks or create materials that are too convincing for safe handling.

However, I can provide a generic, clearly labeled template for a "Login" form that demonstrates the structure of a phishing page without mimicking any specific real-world brand or using realistic banking imagery. This template includes comments highlighting common phishing indicators (such as missing HTTPS, suspicious URLs, and urgent language) which you can use to teach seniors how to spot these red flags.

### Generic Phishing Awareness Demo Template

**HTML (index.html)**
This file contains a generic login form. Notice the comments explaining why this looks suspicious.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Secure Login - Demo Only</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
        }
        .login-container {
            background: white;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
            width: 300px;
            text-align: center;
        }
        /* Red flag: Urgent language often used in phishing */
        .urgent-message {
            color: red;
            font-weight: bold;
            font-size: 14px;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        input {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            margin: 10px 0;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
        }
        button {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            background-color: #007bff;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            cursor: pointer;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #0056b3;
        }
        /* Red flag: Missing secure icon or generic logo */
        .logo-placeholder {
            font-size: 24px;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="login-container">
    <!-- Red flag: Generic logo, not a real bank logo -->
    <div class="logo-placeholder">SECURE BANK DEMO</div>

    <!-- Red flag: Urgent warning to induce panic -->
    <div class="urgent-message">⚠️ ACCOUNT SUSPENDED! VERIFY NOW OR LOSE ACCESS.</div>

    <form action="process.php" method="POST">
        <!-- Red flag: Input names might be misleading -->
        <input type="text" name="username" placeholder="Username" required>
        <input type="password" name="password" placeholder="Password" required>

        <!-- Red flag: Fake verification code request -->
        <input type="text" name="code" placeholder="Enter 6-digit code" required>

        <button type="submit">VERIFY ACCOUNT</button>
    </form>

    <p style="font-size: 12px; color: #666; margin-top: 10px;">
        This is a demo page for educational purposes only.
    </p>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

**PHP (process.php)**
This script safely handles the form submission without storing any sensitive data. It logs the event to a file for demonstration purposes only.

```php
<?php
// process.php
// This script demonstrates safe handling of form data without storing credentials.

// Check if the form was submitted
if ($_SERVER["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST") {

    // Sanitize and validate input (Basic example)
    $username = isset($_POST['username']) ? htmlspecialchars($_POST['username']) : '';
    $code = isset($_POST['code']) ? htmlspecialchars($_POST['code']) : '';

    // In a real scenario, you would validate against a database.
    // Here, we simply log the attempt to demonstrate the flow.

    $logFile = "demo_logs.txt";
    $timestamp = date("Y-m-d H:i:s");

    // Create a log entry that does NOT include the password
    $logEntry = "[$timestamp] Login Attempt - User: $username - Code: $code\n";

    // Append to log file
    file_put_contents($logFile, $logEntry, FILE_APPEND);

    // Display a safe response
    echo "<h2>Request Received</h2>";
    echo "<p>For demonstration purposes, this is a safe environment.</p>";
    echo "<p>Log entry created in <code>demo_logs.txt</code>.</p>";
    echo "<p><strong>Security Note:</strong> Never enter real passwords on unverified sites.</p>";
    echo "<a href='index.html'>Return to Demo</a>";
} else {
    echo "<p>Please load the login form first.</p>";
}
?>
```

### Educational Talking Points for Seniors
When presenting this module, emphasize the following indicators visible in the code and the rendered page:

1.  **Urgency and Fear:** The text "ACCOUNT SUSPENDED! VERIFY NOW" is designed to make the user act quickly without thinking. Real banks rarely use this language in login pages.
2.  **Generic Branding:** The logo is just text ("SECURE BANK DEMO"). Real banks have specific, high-quality logos.
3.  **Request for Extra Information:** Asking for a "6-digit code" alongside the username and password is a common tactic. Banks usually send codes via SMS or email *after* you log in, not as part of the login form itself.
4.  **URL Verification:** Teach users to check the browser address bar. A legitimate bank site will have `https://` and the correct domain name (e.g., `bankname.com`), not a random string of characters or a misspelled domain.

